# File: .gitignore
# Purpose: What is built, not written, and the files that are tracked even
#          when a pattern would otherwise catch them.
#
# ## Why a root file and no others
#
# Cargo puts every workspace member's output in **one** `target/` at the root,
# because the members are one workspace.  A `/target` in each crate ignores a
# directory cargo never creates, and fourteen copies of a rule that cannot fire
# are fourteen things to read before finding the one that matters.
#
# The patterns that matter here are extension and directory-name patterns
# (`*.log`, `*.o`, `*.d`, `__pycache__/`), and git applies those at **every depth**
# from the file that states them, so a crate beside one needs no copy.  What a
# second file would buy is a rule scoped to one directory, and this tree has no
# artifact scoped to one directory: cargo writes one `target/` at the root, and
# `cargo xtask` writes its logs and disks into `target/` or the system's
# temporary directory.
#
# That absence is checked rather than trusted: `scripts/check-repo-integrity.sh`
# refuses a file that is tracked and ignored, and refuses a tracked build
# product whatever the ignore set says.  A crate that ever does gain an artifact
# of its own gets a `.gitignore` beside it, and the gate is what makes the
# difference between "nobody needed one" and "nobody noticed".
#
# ## Why there are `!` rules
#
# A negative rule is how this file says "this one is the product, not the
# output".  It costs a line now and it is the difference between a clone that
# builds and one that does not: a pattern added later that swallowed
# `Cargo.lock` or `.cargo/config.toml` would be a build failure discovered by
# whoever cloned next, and the exception written beside the pattern is the
# thing that stops it.
#
# None of the patterns below currently matches any of these files.  That is the
# point of writing them anyway: they are a **guard**, not a workaround, and
# each names why its file is the product.
#
# The one alternative would be a second target directory: `target-elf/` was
# created once, to link the aarch64 image as an ELF with symbols so a gdb
# session could name addresses (scripts/debug-aarch64-smp.sh rebuilds it when
# it is missing), and it is covered by the rule above rather than by a
# `.gitignore` of its own, for the reason the header states.
#
# ## Why there is no entry for a generated file
#
# This header used to claim one: `arch/x86_64/src/kaslr_relocs.generated.rs`,
# described as generated and tracked because the `kaslr` build `include!`s it.
# The tree has no such file (`find` and `git ls-files` both come up empty), so
# the paragraph was describing a file that does not exist.  It is recorded here
# rather than deleted silently because a reader who remembers it would otherwise
# think the rule had been dropped by mistake.  When the `kaslr` build does write
# one, it belongs in the section above, named, with the reason it must survive.

# Cargo
#
# The workspace's one output directory.  The leading slash anchors it to this
# repository's root, so a path a document cites is not silently swallowed.
/target/

# Cargo's incremental state, when a crate is built outside the workspace.
**/target/debug/incremental/

# `cargo bench` writes criterion's reports here.
**/criterion/

# A second target directory, created once to link the aarch64 image as an ELF
# with symbols so a gdb session could name addresses.  The leading slash of the
# rule above anchors it to the root, which is exactly why a sibling spelling
# needs its own line: a pattern that covered every directory named `target*`
# would be a rule with no reason attached.
/target-elf/

# Build products a document names
#
# `cargo xtask run` writes the serial log and the disk image it boots with.
*.log
*.disk

# An ISO or a disc image built for a gate.
*.iso
*.img

# Object, dependency and disassembly files a tool may drop beside an image.
*.o
*.d
*.dSYM/

# Rust
#
# `cargo fmt` backups and rust-analyzer's cache.
*.rs.bk
.rust-analyzer/
rustc-ice-*.txt

# Python
#
# `tools/extract_tests.py` is the only Python in the tree, and it is a tool
# rather than a package: no build directory, no egg.  What it leaves is a
# bytecode cache, which was committed once before this rule existed.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytype/

# A virtual environment, if a reader makes one to run the tool.
.venv/
venv/

# Editors and agents
#
# The rule is the tool's output, not the tool: a `.vscode/settings.json` a
# reader wants to share is theirs to force in.
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# Operating systems
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# Local, not shared
*.local

# Files that are the product, not the output
#
# Each line says why it has to survive a pattern someone may add later.  A
# `.gitignore` is read top to bottom and the last match wins, so these sit last
# on purpose: a rule added above them cannot shadow them.

# The dependency graph this workspace resolves to.  A binary crate's lock file
# is the build input that makes a clone build the same versions; without it,
# two clones of one commit can build different trees.
!Cargo.lock

# The toolchain the tree is written against.  `rust-toolchain.toml` selects the
# compiler and its components; a clone without it picks whatever the host has.
!rust-toolchain.toml

# Per-target linker scripts and rustflags.  Every bare-metal image depends on
# this file for the address it links at.
!.cargo/config.toml

# The formatting the gate checks.  Without it `cargo fmt --check` measures a
# different style.
!.rustfmt.toml

# Line endings and binary markers, which are what keep a checkout identical
# across hosts.
!.gitattributes
!.gitignore
